{
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "The maximum likelihood estimate $\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ML}}$ is asymptotically unbiased, meaning $\\mathbb{E}[\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ML}}] \\to \\theta$ as $n \\to \\infty$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Citation: [00:22:53\u201300:23:38] - 'This means that theta-ml is a random variable, right? And if it's random variable, we can ask ourselves what would be its expectation and what would be its variance, for example. And we can prove that its expectation is asymptotically unbiased. So it equals the true value of our unknown parameter theta when n is sufficiently large.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The maximum likelihood estimate $\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ML}}$ is consistent, meaning $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ML}} = \\theta$ almost surely.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Citation: [00:24:25\u201300:25:07] - 'if the number of observations n goes to infinity, then we can easily prove, well, not easily, but we can prove, and this was done in statistics courses, that our maximum likelihood estimate converges to the true value when n goes to infinity. And this is, this property is known as consistency.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "For large $n$, $\\sqrt{n}(\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ML}} - \\theta) \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0, I^{-1}(\\theta))$, where $I(\\theta)$ is the Fisher information matrix.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Citation: [00:25:07\u201300:26:16] - 'this has almost a normal distribution with the mean of course the true value this follows from the property number one and with variance that looks like this so this is n times so-called feature information matrix inverse in particular this means that the variance of our maximum value estimator goes to zero when n goes to plus infinity'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The variance of $\\hat{\\theta}_{\\text{ML}}$ is approximately $\\frac{1}{n} I^{-1}(\\theta)$ for large $n$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Citation: [00:25:07\u201300:26:16] - 'this has almost a normal distribution with the mean of course the true value this follows from the property number one and with variance that looks like this so this is n times so-called feature information matrix inverse in particular this means that the variance of our maximum value estimator goes to zero when n goes to plus infinity'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The maximum likelihood estimator achieves the Cram\u00e9r-Rao lower bound for variance among unbiased estimators.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Citation: [00:26:23\u201300:27:59] - 'it appears that maximum likelihood estimate has the smallest possible variance. So it has exactly this lower bound for the variance. So this means that no other unbiased estimate can have smaller variance that maximum likelihood estimator has.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The Cram\u00e9r-Rao lower bound for variance is $\\frac{1}{n} I^{-1}(\\theta)$.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Citation: [00:26:23\u201300:27:59] - 'that n times phi information matrix inverse' (note: 'phi' is likely a mishearing of 'Fisher')"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The maximum likelihood estimator is efficient among unbiased estimators.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Citation: [00:26:23\u201300:27:59] - 'it has exactly this lower bound for the variance. So this means that no other unbiased estimate can have smaller variance that maximum likelihood estimator has.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The Fisher information matrix is referred to as 'feature information matrix' in the ASR transcript.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Citation: [00:25:07\u201300:26:16] - 'so this is n times so-called feature information matrix inverse'"
    }
  ],
  "clarity_1_to_5": 3,
  "caveat": "The ASR has transcription errors: 'Raul Karamer' (should be Cram\u00e9r-Rao), 'feature information matrix' (should be Fisher information matrix), and 'phi' (should be Fisher). These errors are noted in the 'Unclear Terms' section of the provided notes."
}
